// The gunbc-served dashboard instantiation (docs/plans/gunbc-served-dashboard-design.md; belt B,
// parent-approved 2026-07-20). H = RoadmapServeHandler: every row is a DynamicRoute whose handler
// calls the SAME authority the static-site artifacts call (roadmap_page, roadmap_static_site) — the
// artifact table and this serve table are two projections of one authority set, never two copies of
// content, and every body is computed at request time so the served page is the LIVE roadmap, not a
// deploy-frozen snapshot (the section-3 dual-representation cost this module exists to remove).
// Dispatch: POST /dispatch/NODE_ID invokes gunbc.roadmap_belt_actuate.belt_dispatch_node IN-PROCESS
// — the single dispatch authority (#6914), no emitted JS, no subprocess exec, no env-var channel.
// The launch button that POSTs here is transport-agnostic and lands with belt A in roadmap_page
// (lane agreement 2026-07-20): the page never knows which backend answers.

data roadmap_serve_interpreted_scaffold: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: RealizationDispatch,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.roadmap_serve",
    decl_name: "roadmap_serve_handle",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

// ROADMAP section-4 rules a NEW long-running interpreted workload a review reject, routed through
// emit-on-demand. gunbc serve under the tree-walker is exactly that workload, so it lands as this
// DECLARED scaffold: dissolution = emit-on-demand of the serve closure (emit -> build -> run
// native, artifact shared by content-hash — the same lane as the S1 parse census receipt). Interim
// cost is bounded and strictly better than the belt-A alternative it supersedes: A pays a full CLI
// startup (load + compile + resolve) per dispatch click; this process pays it once at bind time.

// THE SOCKET SEAM WAS RESTORED ON 2026-08-19, AND THIS ROW RECORDS WHY THAT IS A REPAIR RATHER THAN
// NEW DEBT. #8286 deleted the cli_run driver surface and took `serve` and `converge` out as ONE
// population; the two are not the same case, and deleting them together is what made it wrong.
// Converge has a successor that EXISTS -- the modeled convergence spine, fleet_converge_timer ->
// fleet_converge_apply -> host_effect_realize -- so its verb can refuse and route there. Serve's
// declared successor is the scaffold ABOVE, roadmap_serve_interpreted_scaffold, dissolving to
// emit-on-demand of the serve closure, and it was never built. So the deletion removed a live
// outward-facing production route with no minimal Y able to hold the boundary, which is precisely
// the case DESIGN section 3 carves out: a gap-intolerant boundary keeps the staged form, and where
// no Y can hold it at all X stays but stays FROZEN. What the restore does is put X back and freeze
// it.
//
// MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, because the cost of the repair turned on one fact. The retained engine
// still evaluates this module completely: roadmap_serve_witness_test runs 16/16 green against a
// binary built from the post-deletion tree, covering route selection, 404 and 501 folding, and
// dispatch-refuses-before-effects. What #8286 removed was the SOCKET SEAM only -- accept, minimal
// HTTP/1.1 parse, response write -- which its own surviving doc comment still described after the
// code was gone. The deletion left the prose and took the machinery, so the restore is a quarry
// revert into comments that already marked the insertion points, not new authoring.
//
// NO SECOND OBLIGATION IS REGISTERED, DELIBERATELY. The scaffold above already binds
// roadmap_serve_handle and already names the correct terminal, so its dissolution IS this seam's
// restoration trigger; a fresh row beside it would be two obligations over one independently
// removable unit, which DESIGN section 5 forbids. FROZEN MEANS: no new options, no new verbs, no
// new routes into the interpreter, no completion work, no flag or environment gate re-opening it --
// an escape hatch is refused outright and would be a NewEscapeHatchOrAdmissionRow under
// gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing besides.
//
// THE DELETED REFUSAL'S SUBSTANTIVE CLAIM IS NOT RETRACTED. Serve remains a desired SERVICE
// OCCURRENCE that convergence should observe and reconcile rather than a command, and it cannot be
// modeled that way today because gunbc.host_effect carries only the LiveDeployApply /
// LiveDeployRetract / LiveDeployEnsureDependency triple and the runner-slot family -- no generic
// service-occurrence member exists. That absence is why BOTH bring-up paths must name a command:
// the systemd unit through gunbc.live_deploy.emit, and the tmux path through
// gunbc.roadmap_dashboard_instance dashboard_instance_serve_argv. Registering that member is
// separate work and is the thing that stops this recurring.
//
// THE ENGINE OBJECTION IS RECORDED RATHER THAN ARGUED AWAY. This does make the interpreter
// load-bearing for a capability two lanes are deleting -- the propagation consequence DESIGN
// section 3 names. It is not a NEW dependency; it is the one that stood until #8286. But if this
// route begins attracting completion work or performance work on the interpreted loop, the freeze
// has been repealed by drift, which is the standing danger gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing
// v1_seed_standing already warns about for the active-maintenance arm.

type RoadmapServeHandler
  = RoadmapDailyPageHandler
  | RoadmapFullPageHandler
  | FleetStandingPageHandler
  | RoadmapMdHandler
  | DispatchJsonHandler
  | HealthzHandler
  | DashboardInstanceHandler
  | ProviderReadinessHandler
  | BeltDispatchHandler
  | DispatchAssetHandler
  | SandboxPageHandler
  | InstrumentSandboxPageHandler
  | InstrumentTunerPageHandler
  | InstrumentTunerAssetHandler
  | InstrumentMotionPageHandler
  | InstrumentMotionAssetHandler
  | RepoAtlasPageHandler
  | SandboxAssetHandler
  | SandboxEchoHandler
  | SessionsJsonHandler
  | AttemptsJsonHandler
  | WorkflowJsonHandler
  | ObservationJsonHandler
  | BeltStopHandler
  | BeltPublishHandler
  | ApprovalConfirmPageHandler
  | ApprovalDecideHandler
  | ApprovalSubmitHandler
  | ApprovalStatusHandler
  | IssueDetailPageHandler
  | IssueAssignHandler
  | IssueCommentHandler

// Fail-closed table construction (review 40286, the §5 catch: the earlier serve_tpl mapped
// MalformedPathTemplate to an EMPTY template, and an empty template matches exactly '/' — so a typo
// in any route literal would have silently become a second root matcher, a widen at the very seam
// §5 forbids). The build is now a typed fold: the FIRST malformed literal refuses the WHOLE table
// (RoadmapServeTableRefused carries raw/segment/reason — typed, located), and the serve entry
// answers EVERY request with a 500 naming the broken literal until it is fixed — a loudly broken
// dashboard, never silently wrong routing. The RED control is
// witness_malformed_route_literal_refuses_table (a bad literal through the same build fold ->
// Refused).

type RoadmapServeRouteSpec {
  method: HttpMethod
  raw: String
  handler: RoadmapServeHandler
}

type RoadmapServeTableBuild
  = RoadmapServeTable { table: ServedRouteTable<RoadmapServeHandler> }
  | RoadmapServeTableRefused { raw: String, segment: String, reason: String }

// THE APPROVAL PAIR IS TWO ROUTES ON PURPOSE. The operator's tap is a GET, and a GET must not
// spend the decision -- link previews, mail scanners, chat unfurlers and browser prefetch all
// fetch URLs nobody has looked at. So the notification's click target is the GET, which renders
// what is being decided and nothing else; the button on that page POSTs to the same path, and
// that request is the only one that commits. Same capability in both, different consequence, and
// the split is what keeps "opened the link" distinguishable from "decided".
// THE SERVED PATH IS ONE FACT. gunbc.roadmap_site_surface_render already declared each of these
// paths as data -- the static site, the surface-observation bundle and the deploy witnesses all
// cite those constants -- while these rows carried a second spelling of the same string. Two copies
// of a path agree until one moves, and the one that moves silently is the one nothing reads back:
// a witness spelling "/roadmap.md" reaches a 404 and asserts nothing (review 63317, and the
// operator's live check against the running instance). The rows now consume the declaration, so a
// renamed route moves the routing table, the static artifact and every witness together.
// THE ONE FACT THE SERVING PROCESS CANNOT OBSERVE, DECLARED SO IT CAN REFUSE.
//
// Route / is served from a body the BELT TICK rendered, under whatever plan that process held. The
// stored body carries no identity of the authority it came from, so a serving process handed a
// plan cannot check whether the body agrees with it -- the class is filed as
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_prerendered_body_carries_no_identity_of_its_source.
//
// That made this entry's plan parameter a constructor for silent wrongness: before the parameter
// existed no caller COULD diverge, and DESIGN 4b puts silent wrongness outside the ladder and
// forbids it outright rather than admitting it at a low rung. A note saying so is not a refusal
// (4c), so the honest arm available today is the one 5 names: refuse, never widen.
//
// The agreement is therefore a DECLARED fact of the call rather than something inferred. A caller
// whose own tick renders the daily body under the same plan says so and is served; a caller that
// cannot say so gets a located refusal on that ONE route, and every other route still answers from
// its plan. This is 4b's "outside the modeled guarantee" column handled at a declared boundary --
// observed, refused, or mitigated, never fabricated -- not a fifth rung and not a proof.

type ServedDailyBodyAuthority
  = DailyBodyRenderedUnderThisPlan
  | DailyBodyAuthorityUnknown

